<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Anglo-Dutch War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1652">1652</a>–<a href="/years/1654">1654</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-wars">Anglo-Dutch Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Anglo-Dutch War of 1652–1654 was a naval war between the Commonwealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> over trade and shipping, sharpened by the English Navigation Act of 1651. It was fought almost entirely at sea, in battles such as the Kentish Knock, Portland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheveningen">Scheveningen</a>. The Treaty of Westminster of 1654 ended it on terms favourable to England.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-anglo-dutch-war.jpg" alt="First Anglo-Dutch War" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Abrahamsz Beerstraaten · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Scheveningen_(Slag_bij_Ter_Heijde)(Jan_Abrahamsz._Beerstraten).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> had supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the Eighty Years' War. The separate peace between England and Spain in 1604 strained the relationship, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, having made peace with Spain in 1648, replaced the English as the dominant traders with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, adding to an English resentment of Dutch trade that had grown since 1590. By the middle of the century the Dutch had the largest merchant fleet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, with more ships than all other states combined, and had conquered most of Portugal's territories and trading posts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>.</p><p>Tensions rose when the English Parliament passed the Navigation Act 1651, which required cargo to English ports and colonies to be carried in English ships. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> saw acceptance as subordinating their trade to the English system. The more immediate cause was action by the English navy and privateers: 140 Dutch merchantmen were seized in 1651, and another 30 in January 1652 alone. An English embassy under Oliver St John, which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> on 7 March 1651 proposing a confederation of the two Commonwealths, left on 2 July without an alliance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Both nations were unready, and war might have been delayed but for an encounter between the fleets of Lieutenant-Admiral Maarten Tromp and General at Sea Robert Blake near Dover on 29 May 1652. Cromwell's ordinance required foreign fleets to dip their flag in salute. The salute was not given promptly, and Blake opened fire in the brief Battle of Dover; Tromp lost two ships but escorted his convoy to safety. The Grand Pensionary Adriaan Pauw went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in a last attempt to prevent war, but English demands had become extreme. Parliament declared war on 10 July 1652. Despite a series of victories in 1652 and 1653, the Commonwealth could not blockade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> trade, although English privateers inflicted serious losses on Dutch merchant shipping.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The economic damage led to the Treaty of Westminster in 1654, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> made minor concessions. They accepted the Navigation Act, paid an indemnity of £85,000 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s losses and agreed to salute English warships in the Channel. The Dutch realised few of their own aims. Cromwell dropped his aim of a union subordinating the Dutch and asked instead that the House of Orange be excluded from the stadtholdership. In a secret annexe, the States of Holland passed an Act of Seclusion in May 1654. The commercial issues remained unresolved, and in 1665 they led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Anglo-Dutch War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…lawyer Adriaen van der Donck, condemning the company's mismanagement and demanding full rights as citizens of the Netherlands; after three years' wrangling the Dutch government granted a measure of self-government and recalled Stuyvesant in April 1652, orders rescinded a month later when the <b>First Anglo-Dutch War</b> broke out.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The regime passed the Navigation Act of 1651, fought the <b>First Anglo-Dutch War</b> and allowed some religious toleration while repressing Levellers and royalists.</blockquote><a href="/state/commonwealth-of-england">Commonwealth of England</a></li></ul></section>
